# G027 — Fortune Teller

*v2. See `fortune-teller.md` for prose: constrained nondeterminism
(random within a category), context-independent response as the
anti-pattern worth naming, stratified sampling (space/stratum/
selection), the composition of alarm + category + injection.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Daily Inspiration System

[daily-inspiration
    IN ORDER TO
        fire a morning alarm that selects a category-bounded fortune
        and inject it into today's daily note
        composing alarm + stratified sampling + content insertion

    @teller <- [fortune[new]]
    [fortune[seed-pool {teller: @teller,
                         category: "motivation",
                         fortunes: [...]}]]
    [fortune[seed-pool {teller: @teller,
                         category: "wisdom",
                         fortunes: [...]}]]
    [fortune[seed-pool {teller: @teller,
                         category: "caution",
                         fortunes: [...]}]]

    at [[07:00]] [
        @day-of-week <- [clock[day-of-week]]
        @category <- @day-of-week
            where "Monday"    -> "motivation"
            where "Wednesday" -> "wisdom"
            where "Friday"    -> "caution"
            otherwise         -> "wisdom"

        @fortune <- [fortune[draw
                               {teller: @teller,
                                category: @category}]]

        [daily-note[prepend
                     {path: [[today]] + ".md",
                      section: "Fortune",
                      content: @fortune}]]
    ]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[fortune
    new
    seed-pool         {teller, category, fortunes}
    draw              {teller, category}           ;; stratified random
    ask-question      {teller, question}           ;; context-independent
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: constrained nondeterminism `where` category picks the stratum

[fortune[draw {teller: @t, category: @cat}]
    IN ORDER TO
        select randomly within the @cat pool
        so agency lives in the productive middle — bounded choice,
        not chaos (unbounded) and not determinism (single answer)

    @pool <- @t:pools at @cat
        where present and count is at least 1
            -> select one uniformly at random from @pool
        otherwise
            -> #category-empty @cat
]

Three levels of the same concept: the fortune space, the category
stratum, the drawn sample. Same primitive statistical sampling uses;
same primitive G024's random-by-tag uses a simpler form of.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: context-independent response as named anti-pattern

[fortune[ask-question {teller: @t, question: @q}]
    ignores @q
    returns [fortune[draw {teller: @t, category: "wisdom"}]]
    IN ORDER TO
        name the anti-pattern explicitly — agents that listen but do
        not respond to input. Good agent design threads context into
        the response; fortune tellers refuse to.
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @t <- [fortune[new]]
    [fortune[seed-pool
              {teller: @t, category: "wisdom",
               fortunes: ["Trust the process.",
                          "Simplicity over cleverness.",
                          "What gets measured gets managed."]}]]

    -> [fortune[draw {teller: @t, category: "wisdom"}]]
    ;; -> one of the three, uniformly at random

    -> [fortune[ask-question
                  {teller: @t, question: "Will the market rise?"}]]
    ;; -> a wisdom fortune, context-independent
]
